# Find Target Indices After Sorting Array

**Problem:** [LeetCode 2089 – Find Target Indices After Sorting Array](https://leetcode.com/problems/find-target-indices-after-sorting-array/)

## Approach: Sorting + Binary Search

### Theory
First, sort the array in ascending order. Then use `lower_bound()` to find the first position where the target can appear and `upper_bound()` to find the first position greater than the target.

All indices between these two positions contain the target, so store them in the result vector and return it.

### Code
```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> targetIndices(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        vector<int> res;

        int low = lower_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), target) - nums.begin();
        int high = upper_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), target) - nums.begin();

        for (int i = low; i < high; i++) {
            res.push_back(i);
        }

        return res;
    }
};
```

### Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** `O(n log n)` — Sorting takes `O(n log n)`, binary searches take `O(log n)`, and collecting the indices takes `O(k)`, where `k` is the number of occurrences of the target.
- **Auxiliary Space:** `O(1)` excluding sorting-related stack space.
- **Result Space:** `O(k)` for storing the target indices.

### Key Learnings
- Using `sort()` to arrange elements in ascending order.
- Using `lower_bound()` to find the first occurrence position.
- Using `upper_bound()` to find the position immediately after the last occurrence.
- Handling duplicate elements efficiently with binary search.
